        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most riders, the Wahoo KICKR CORE 2 is the smart trainer to buy</strong>:
            it fits most modern bikes, ships ready to ride, connects over Wi-Fi and costs a little over half
            the flagship price. Step up to the Wahoo KICKR for more power and a heavier flywheel, or the Tacx
            NEO 2T for 25% climbs and near-silent running. The KICKR ROLLR is the answer if you never want to
            remove a wheel, and the Saris Fluid2 is a sound budget trainer as long as you do not need the app
            to control it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, check your axle, gear count and freehub before ordering, budget for a
            cassette if one is not included, and put a mat and a fan in the room. If a trainer on your own
            bike is not the right fit after all,
